                    <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelCatalog" native="true">
                     <property name="geometry">
                      <rect>
                       <x>0</x>
                       <y>0</y>
                       <width>1</width>
                       <height>1</height>
                      </rect>
                     </property>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_izhikevich" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Neuron dynamics · Izhikevich</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Adapted from Izhikevich (2003); firmware and desktop emulator have separate implementations.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Firmware/Spikeling_V3/Core_functions.h</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;From a compact model to diverse firing patterns&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Izhikevich model couples a membrane-voltage variable v to a recovery variable u. Changing a, b, c and d produces different firing behaviours, including adaptation and bursting.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Spikeling connects this model to current injection, light-sensitive input, noise and two synaptic inputs. The neuron generator lets learners relate parameters to observable behaviour.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;With identical stimulation, how does changing recovery alter the first response and the later firing rate?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Core equations&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;296&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;\frac{dv}{dt}=0.04v^2+5v+140-u+I&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;150&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;\frac{du}{dt}=a(bv-u)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At the spike cutoff:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;196&quot; height=&quot;29&quot; alt=&quot;v\leftarrow c,\qquad u\leftarrow u+d&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Variables and parameters&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;v: model membrane potential; u: recovery variable; a: recovery time-scale parameter; b: coupling to voltage; c: reset voltage; d: recovery increment after a spike.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I is the summed model input. Current-clamp inputs use model units; do not infer a physical current calibration from these equations alone. The spike cutoff is a numerical event rule, not a complete biological initiation mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Core_functions.h implements the hardware-facing update and output logic. Izhikevich_parameters.h contains neuron presets; General_settings.h contains timing and state settings. Graph_Emulator.py implements the desktop simulation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Model integration time and elapsed wall-clock time are different quantities. Record the timing settings and the chosen hardware or emulator route when comparing experiments.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Optional pharmacology and voltage clamp add separate paths. The two core equations do not by themselves describe every optional mode.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a phenomenological spiking model. The recovery variable is not a direct measurement of one ion-channel population, and the reset rule does not reproduce a complete action-potential waveform.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It is well suited to exploring excitability, adaptation and coding. It does not establish preparation-specific ion-channel kinetics, dendritic morphology or quantitative drug responses.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[[&quot;izhikevich&quot;, &quot;adapted&quot;], [&quot;spikeling&quot;, &quot;background&quot;]]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_synapses" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Synapses &amp; short-term plasticity</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Educational implementation; event-driven, simplified Tsodyks–Markram-style resource model.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Firmware/Spikeling_V3/STP_functions.h</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Timing and recent activity shape synaptic responses&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Each synapse combines a signed gain with a decaying response. Positive and negative gain support excitation and inhibition. Short-term plasticity changes event strength according to recent presynaptic activity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Off, combined, facilitation and depression modes are independently selected for the two synapses.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;How does the second response change as the interval between two presynaptic spikes increases?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Event-driven state updates&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Between events separated by Δt:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;246&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;u\leftarrow U+(u-U)\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_F}\right)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;242&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;x\leftarrow 1-(1-x)\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_D}\right)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Event multiplier:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;86&quot; height=&quot;36&quot; alt=&quot;M=\frac{u}{U}x&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;After the event, depletion updates the resource fraction and facilitation updates utilisation, when enabled:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;321&quot; height=&quot;29&quot; alt=&quot;x\leftarrow x(1-u),\qquad u\leftarrow u+U(1-u)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;U: baseline utilisation; x: available resource fraction; τF and τD: recovery time constants in ms. Disabled processes follow their explicit branches in the implementation.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;STP_functions.h updates the states at incoming spike events using simulated elapsed time. The normalisation makes the fully recovered first response equal to the original gain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Core_functions.h combines the event multiplier with the synaptic input. Pharmacology_functions.h contains the separate optional probabilistic-release route. Stochastic release and short-term resource dynamics are distinct mechanisms.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The resource variables are effective states, not direct measurements of vesicle pools. Preset values are teaching choices. Short-term facilitation and depression do not imply long-term potentiation or depression.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Analogue presynaptic voltage and digital spike events represent different input routes. Interpret the response in the context of the route actually used.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[[&quot;spikeling&quot;, &quot;background&quot;]]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_clamp" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Current clamp &amp; voltage clamp</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Educational implementation; passive circuit and separate active conductance-based membrane.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Firmware/Spikeling_V3/VoltageClamp_functions.h</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Two ways of asking a neuron a question&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Current clamp changes the input current and observes voltage. Voltage clamp sets a voltage command and reports the current required by the clamp model.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The passive membrane exposes leak and capacitive behaviour. The active membrane adds voltage-dependent currents. The CC/VC selector changes the meaning of the patch control.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Which parts of a voltage-step response remain after switching from an active to a passive membrane?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Passive circuit relationships&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;145&quot; height=&quot;41&quot; alt=&quot;I_{\mathrm{leak}}=\frac{V_m-E_{\mathrm{leak}}}{R_m}&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;162&quot; height=&quot;42&quot; alt=&quot;I_{\mathrm{access}}=\frac{V_{\mathrm{cmd}}-V_m}{R_s}&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The membrane charge balance includes capacitance, leak, applied inputs and any enabled conductance terms.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;With mV and MΩ, the resistance expressions yield nA. The implementation explicitly converts capacitance and time units. Read its current signs and exported sample fields before interpreting the recorded total.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The active path adds sodium and potassium terms; it is not obtained by imposing a command voltage on the Izhikevich reset equations.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;VoltageClamp_functions.h contains the passive and active cells, ideal/access modes, current limits and numerical stepping. Core_functions.h routes the command, inputs and exported sample values.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;External stimulation remains part of the experimental input route. The About page never sends clamp commands or changes a running experiment.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Access resistance and current limits can prevent Vm from reaching the command. Check the mode and saturation before interpreting a response.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The active VC membrane and current-clamp Izhikevich model are different representations. Their responses should not be treated as measurements from an identical detailed biological cell.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_pharmacology" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Pharmacology &amp; probabilistic release</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Phenomenological educational extension; not a preparation-specific pharmacokinetic model.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Firmware/Spikeling_V3/Pharmacology_functions.h</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Use perturbations to test a mechanism&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The optional module includes TTX, TEA, apamin, 4-AP, NBQX, gabazine, APV and GVIA. Their actions are represented through effective availability, intrinsic excitability, synaptic and release terms.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Probabilistic release introduces trial-to-trial variation at incoming presynaptic events.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Can a smaller postsynaptic response be explained by weaker release, altered receptors or changed intrinsic excitability? Which control experiment would distinguish them?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Effective availability and release&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The implementation precomputes Hill-type concentration-response blockade. Concentration units and target mapping are drug-specific and must be taken from the corresponding parameter definitions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For N release sites with probability p, the binomial release variable K has the following mean and variance. The delivered event is scaled in the implementation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;326&quot; height=&quot;30&quot; alt=&quot;\mathrm{E}[K]=Np,\qquad \mathrm{Var}[K]=Np(1-p)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The random seed controls the pseudo-random sequence. Identical seeds reproduce a sequence only with the same code, state and event ordering.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pharmacology_functions.h owns drug concentrations, blockade, intrinsic states, receptor currents and release state. Serial command registration remains in Serial_functions.h.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The firmware can infer receptor identity from the effective signed synaptic gain. CC and active VC use different membrane representations; consult both paths when interpreting an effect.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A named simulated drug is a teaching perturbation. Its concentration-response curve is not a prediction of the dose required for a particular biological preparation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Effects depend on enabled pathways, synaptic sign and membrane mode. Absence of a visible change does not establish absence of a biological target. The original Spikeling publication does not validate these later extensions.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_imaging" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Calcium &amp; fluorescence</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Adapted forward-model concepts; distinguish calcium dynamics, indicator response and acquisition.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Software/GUI - PyQt6-PySide6/Graph_Imaging.py</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;From spikes to an indirect optical readout&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Vm → detected spikes → calcium dynamics → indicator response → frame-sampled fluorescence.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Spikes are detected using an upward voltage-threshold crossing and a refractory interval. Calcium and indicator states are updated from incoming samples; fluorescence is observed at camera-frame boundaries and held between frames.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Compare a spike train and its fluorescence at two frame rates. Which events remain distinguishable, and which merge?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Calcium rise/decay kernel&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;117&quot; height=&quot;40&quot; alt=&quot;\tau_{dr}=\frac{\tau_d\tau_r}{\tau_d+\tau_r}&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here, s is the spike indicator.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;224&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;x_d\leftarrow x_d\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_d}\right)+A\,s&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;238&quot; height=&quot;38&quot; alt=&quot;x_{dr}\leftarrow x_{dr}\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_{dr}}\right)+A\,s&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The term η represents calcium noise.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;330&quot; height=&quot;30&quot; alt=&quot;C=\max(0,C_{\mathrm{baseline}}+x_d-x_{dr}+\eta)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Calcium is expressed in µM; these time constants and Δt are in ms.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Indicator and fluorescence&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Equilibrium Hill option:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;109&quot; height=&quot;42&quot; alt=&quot;S=\frac{C^n}{C^n+K_d^n}&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Optional kinetic binding, with time in seconds:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;296&quot; height=&quot;41&quot; alt=&quot;\frac{dS_b}{dt}=k_{\mathrm{on}}(S_{\mathrm{total}}-S_b)C^n-k_{\mathrm{off}}S_b&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Linear observation:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;289&quot; height=&quot;30&quot; alt=&quot;F_{\mathrm{mean}}=\mathrm{offset}+\mathrm{gain}\,\alpha(C+\beta)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Saturating observation:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;336&quot; height=&quot;32&quot; alt=&quot;F_{\mathrm{mean}}=\mathrm{offset}+\mathrm{gain}(1+d_{\mathrm{FF,max}}S)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;gain includes optical scaling and the bleaching factor. Observation noise is added at frame times.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Imaging scope handles spike detection, calcium dynamics, indicator response, frame sampling and observation noise. The GECI preset table supplies indicator parameters.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The displayed ΔF/F0 uses an offset-corrected reference based on baseline calcium and gain. The baseline reference excludes stochastic noise and the dynamic bleaching factor, so bleaching can remain visible as drift.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Wei-style S2F concepts inform the rise/decay kernel; Vogelstein-style concepts inform the linear observation route. These are adaptations, not complete reproductions of the cited inference methods. The optional binding ODE is documented from the code; this bibliography does not assign an unverified paper to it.&lt;/p&gt;
</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Indicator presets combine effective kinetics and biochemical quantities that depend on assay conditions. A preset name does not imply quantitative validation of every parameter or the entire simulated optical trace.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The simulator uses a reduced calcium and optical model. It does not simulate spatial calcium diffusion, full photon statistics, microscope optics or a preparation-specific camera.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Clamping of states, threshold-based spike detection and finite input sampling affect the result. The saturating expression relative to apo fluorescence should not be confused with ΔF/F0 relative to the chosen nonzero calcium baseline.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[[&quot;wei&quot;, &quot;adapted&quot;], [&quot;vogelstein&quot;, &quot;inspired&quot;], [&quot;chen&quot;, &quot;background&quot;], [&quot;dana&quot;, &quot;background&quot;]]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_extracellular" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Extracellular signals &amp; tetrodes</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Inspired by recording principles; reduced waveform and geometric projection surrogate.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Software/GUI - PyQt6-PySide6/Graph_ExtraCellular.py</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;One neuron, different views across contacts&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The extracellular simulator transforms membrane activity and detected spike times into a four-contact tetrode recording. Geometry changes the contribution of a source to each channel.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Template and derivative-based modes let learners explore waveform differences, noise, reference choice and the information available for spike sorting.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Move a source relative to the tetrode. Does its largest channel remain the same, and how does that affect unit identification?&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Reduced forward-model structure&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Channel i combines geometrically weighted source waveforms, independent noise, shared noise and hum.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;380&quot; height=&quot;53&quot; alt=&quot;y_i(t)=\sum_j w_{ij}s_j(t)+\eta_i(t)+\eta_{\mathrm{shared}}(t)+h(t)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Template mode uses a canonical extracellular waveform at spike events. The alternative uses a smoothed voltage derivative gated around detected spikes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Optional common-average referencing subtracts the contact mean. Display bandpass filtering is part of the recording chain. Exact weighting, filtering and normalisation are defined in Graph_ExtraCellular.py.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Graph_ExtraCellular.py constructs source waveforms, projects them onto four contacts and applies the recording chain. Tetrode.py provides the spatial interface.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gold et al. provides scientific background for geometry-dependent extracellular waveforms. Spikeling does not implement that paper’s full morphology-based forward solution.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;A membrane-voltage derivative is an educational surrogate, not the full extracellular field generated by distributed transmembrane currents.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The reduced sources do not establish realistic morphology, tissue conductivity or absolute extracellular amplitudes. Use the simulation to reason about geometry, overlap and measurement limitations.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[[&quot;gold&quot;, &quot;background&quot;]]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                     <widget class="QWidget" name="About_modelEntry_analysis" native="true">
                      <property name="geometry">
                       <rect>
                        <x>0</x>
                        <y>0</y>
                        <width>100</width>
                        <height>30</height>
                       </rect>
                      </property>
                      <property name="title" stdset="0">
                       <string>Stimulation, recording &amp; analysis</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="provenance" stdset="0">
                       <string>Experimental workflow and software methods; interpretation depends on timing and configuration.</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="source" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">Software/GUI - PyQt6-PySide6/Page_Spikeling_DataAnalysis.py</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="overview" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Turn an observation into a reproducible experiment&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Define a stimulus, select the model and mode, record the response, inspect signal quality, and choose an analysis that answers the experimental question.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Keep the waveform, loop count, parameters, software/firmware versions and time convention alongside each recording.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h3&gt;A question to explore&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Does a larger response reflect a change in firing rate, spike timing or trial reliability? Choose a measure before comparing conditions.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="equations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Useful definitions&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mean firing rate uses the detected spike count and observation duration.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;96&quot; height=&quot;42&quot; alt=&quot;\bar{r}=\frac{N_{\mathrm{spikes}}}{T}&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Trial mean at aligned time t:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;data:image/png;base64,&quot; width=&quot;157&quot; height=&quot;64&quot; alt=&quot;\bar{y}(t)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} y_i(t)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These are definitions, not promises that every analysis option uses an identical detector or time window. Inspect threshold, refractory settings, alignment and units in the selected analysis route.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="implementation" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Implementation in Spikeling&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Page_StimulusGenerator.py defines custom stimulus workflows. Graph_Spikeling.py manages neuron recordings, and Page_Spikeling_DataAnalysis.py handles the analysis view.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The imaging and extracellular views have their own transformation and recording paths. The About documentation records the reviewed source revision; it does not identify the firmware flashed onto a connected board.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="limitations" stdset="0">
                       <string>&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sampling, missed events, interrupted streams, trial alignment and selected analysis windows can change conclusions. Repeated measurements require the same stimulus and a clearly defined comparison.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Exporting a CSV alone may not preserve every experimental setting. Retain configuration and provenance alongside the data. This About update does not add an experiment-configuration export feature.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
                      </property>
                      <property name="refs" stdset="0">
                       <string notr="true">[[&quot;spikeling&quot;, &quot;background&quot;]]</string>
                      </property>
                     </widget>
                    </widget>
                   </widget>
